;; FH-ROOT-004 — the frame is settled before React exists.
;;
;; The ordering is the whole claim. A view body that reads a subscription on
;; its FIRST render must find a frame that is already live and already
;; seeded — not one an effect creates after the first paint, which would
;; render `nil` once and correct itself, and would do it differently under
;; StrictMode, streaming, or a slow first commit.
;;
;; So the proof is the first rendered text. `counter` reads `[:root/n]` and
;; prints it; the plan's `:initial-events` seed that value. If preflight ran
;; late the DOM would say the empty read, and no amount of settling would
;; make that first frame right again.
;;
;; The two `:frame` spellings are two LIFETIMES, and the difference is what
;; teardown later acts on: an ENSURE plan (a make-frame opts map carrying
;; `:id`) is a frame the root owns, a SCOPE reference (a bare frame-id
;; keyword) is one it borrows.
;;
;; The conflict arm is the isolation claim: a second root arriving with a
;; DIFFERENT config for one frame fails, before install and before React,
;; and the installed frame — plus the sibling root already rendering against
;; it — is untouched.
{:fh/id "FH-ROOT-004"
 :fh/law
 "A root's frame plan runs to completion before the host root exists — the frame is live and its initial events drained by the time the first render reads a subscription — the ENSURE and SCOPE spellings own and borrow the frame's lifetime respectively, and a plan meeting one frame under a different config fingerprint fails that root with :rf.error/frame-payload-conflict before install and before React, with the installed frame and its sibling roots untouched."

 :view :counter
 :query [:root/n]

 ;; ENSURE — the root owns the frame. The seed rides `:initial-events`, so
 ;; a preflight that ran late would show the un-seeded read instead.
 :ensure {:frame-id :fh.root/preflight
          :seeded   7
          :first-render-text "7"}

 ;; SCOPE — the root borrows a frame something else created.
 :scope {:frame-id :fh.root/borrowed
         :seeded   11
         :first-render-text "11"}

 ;; A keyword :frame naming no live frame is a configuration error, not a
 ;; silent frameless subtree.
 :absent-scope {:frame-id :fh.root/never-created
                :error    :rf.error/frame-provider-frame-absent}

 ;; The conflict. A second root's ENSURE plan for the same frame under a
 ;; different config fingerprint fails THAT root.
 :conflict {:error :rf.error/frame-payload-conflict
            ;; and the incumbent is untouched — same frame, same value, same
            ;; rendered text, still exactly one live root
            :incumbent-text "7"
            :live-roots-after 1}

 ;; The rejected root left nothing: no host root on its container, no
 ;; registry entry, no second ledger record.
 :rejected-container-children 0
 :ledger-entries-after 1

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; The preflight is also an OWNERSHIP BOUNDARY, and it has two sides.
 ;;
 ;; BEFORE it, nothing has been written — so everything that can still
 ;; fail on the shape of the call belongs there, and a rejection has
 ;; nothing to undo. Building the root's element is the last such step,
 ;; and a bad props slot is the cheapest way to make it fail.
 ;;
 ;; AFTER it, the document is no longer the one the mount was admitted
 ;; against: `:initial-events` are arbitrary application code, and code
 ;; that runs before React can mount a root, unmount one, or take the
 ;; container. So the claim is re-asserted, and a mount whose ground
 ;; moved refuses instead of clobbering what moved onto it.
 ;;
 ;; Both refusals are LOUD and both give back exactly what the attempt
 ;; took — never a sibling's frame reference, and never the incumbent's.
 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 :boundary
 {;; Before: the root form's head is a declared view, so identity and
  ;; the claims resolve; `nil` in the props slot then fails when the
  ;; element is built. Preflight never ran, so there is no frame, no
  ;; ledger record, no claim and no DOM — and the corrected call
  ;; immediately after is an ordinary successful mount.
  :bad-props {:frame-id           :fh.root/boundary-bad-props
              :seeded             7
              :error              :rf.error/view-bad-props
              :frame-live         false
              :ledger-entries     0
              :live-roots         0
              :container-children 0
              :retry-text         "7"}

  ;; After, fresh root: the plan mounts a root into THIS mount's own
  ;; container. The inner root is live and registered; the outer attempt
  ;; refuses rather than allocate a second host root over it.
  :re-entrant {:frame-id       :fh.root/boundary-re-entrant
               :inner-root-id  :fh.root/boundary-inner
               :error          :rf.error/root-container-in-use
               :live-root-ids  #{:fh.root/boundary-inner}
               :ledger-entries 0
               :frame-live     false}

  ;; After, live root: the plan unmounts the very root this re-mount was
  ;; admitted to re-render. The captured handle names a host root React
  ;; has already discarded.
  :superseded {:frame-id       :fh.root/boundary-superseded
               :error          :rf.error/root-not-live
               :live-roots     0
               :ledger-entries 0
               :frame-live     false}

  ;; And the other direction, which matters at least as much: an
  ;; ORDINARY re-mount — same id, same container, same plan — is still
  ;; admitted, still re-renders, and still does not re-seed. A fence
  ;; strict enough to refuse this would be worse than the defect.
  ;;
  ;; "Did not re-seed" is counted rather than inferred: one setup step
  ;; increments a counter, and the count after the re-mount is still one.
  ;; The rendered text cannot tell the two apart — a replayed seed writes
  ;; the same value back.
  :idempotent {:frame-id       :fh.root/boundary-idempotent
               :seeded         7
               :text           "7"
               :setup-runs     1
               :live-roots     1
               :ledger-entries 1
               :frame-live     true}}}
